---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="How a Heatmap Can Mislead You"
  description="How a grid-based heatmap can suggest different best locations without changing the underlying demand"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Imagine you want to open a coffee shop. Choosing the right place is not
        a small detail. It can be the difference between a busy shop and a very
        expensive mistake.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So instead of guessing, you use a map. You add every clue you can find:
        searches for coffee, office locations, busy areas, and nearby coffee
        shops. But at first, it all looks like noise.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To make the map easier to read, you place a grid over the map and count
        how many signals fall inside each cell. And suddenly, you get a clear
        answer. This area wins. This is where you should open.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        You almost have the place. You are almost ready to sign. But then one
        question changes everything: Why did you put the grid exactly there?
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        You move the grid just a little bit. The points are the same. The city
        is the same. But now the map recommends a different place.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        And that is the trap. You didn't find the best area. You found the best
        area for that grid.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This is called the Modifiable Areal Unit Problem. When you turn real
        points into artificial areas, your conclusions can depend on the size,
        the shape, and the position of those areas.
      </p>
      <p>
        And yes, the same happens with postal code areas. They are also
        artificial.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Follow me for the full video, where we will see how to reduce the risk
        before making decisions with maps.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
